.TH FL-DISKS 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.4" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
fl-disks \- disk and partition manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfl-disks\fR [\fB\-\-list\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows the disks, their partition tables and partitions, and changes them:
new partition tables (GPT or MBR), new partitions, deleting, formatting,
resizing and moving partitions, partition names and flags, mounting and
unmounting, backing a whole disk up to an image file and writing an image
(.img or .iso) to a disk, such as a USB stick.
.PP
.B Disks is an advanced tool.
Creating, deleting, resizing or formatting partitions can erase all the data
on a drive, and a mistake with the drive the system runs from can leave the
computer unable to start. Back up first. fl-disks says so when it starts
(until "Don't show this again" is chosen), and every change that loses data
asks again and waits for "I understand" to be ticked.
.PP
The layout is read with \fBlsblk\fR(8), without administrator rights. The bar
above the list shows each partition by size, colored by file system, with
the used part of mounted file systems; click a partition or free space to
select it. Drive health comes from the SMART data UDisks reads (ATA and
NVMe); a drive that reports it is failing is shown in red.
.PP
Changes are made by \fBfl-disks-helper\fR, run through pkexec (it asks for
an administrator password), with \fBparted\fR(8) for the partition table and
\fBmkfs.ext4\fR, \fBmkfs.btrfs\fR, \fBmkfs.xfs\fR, \fBmkfs.fat\fR,
\fBmkfs.exfat\fR, \fBmkfs.ntfs\fR or \fBmkswap\fR for file systems (the ones
not installed are greyed out). Partitions are placed on 1 MiB boundaries.
Nothing that is mounted, used as swap or held by another device (LUKS, LVM,
RAID) is changed: unmount it first.
.PP
Resizing: ext4 (unmounted, with \fBe2fsck\fR and \fBresize2fs\fR) and NTFS
(\fBntfsresize\fR) grow and shrink, Btrfs grows and shrinks through a
temporary mount, XFS only grows; FAT, exFAT and swap cannot be resized.
Shrinking shrinks the file system first, growing grows the partition first.
Only a partition without a file system can be moved; to move one with files,
back it up, delete it and create it again where it should be.
.PP
Images are read and written by the helper with the rights of the user who
started fl-disks, so it cannot read or overwrite other people's files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
Prints the disks, partitions and free space and quits.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBFLD_DISKS_SHOW_LOOP\fR=1
Also show loop devices (for trying fl-disks on an image file attached with
\fBlosetup \-P\fR). The helper changes loop devices only when run directly as
root with \fBFLD_DISKS_ALLOW_LOOP\fR=1, never through pkexec.
.SH FILES
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/disks.ini\fR: [General] HideWarning
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBparted\fR(8), \fBlsblk\fR(8), \fBudisksctl\fR(1), \fBfl-files\fR(1), \fBfl-settings\fR(1)
